<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      Lock files, so a restore resolves the same graph every time.

      Every project floats at least one dependency (Version="9.*", "10.*", "4.*"), and with no lock
      file each restore re-resolves them — including the release build, which restores fresh. That
      means the shipped identity provider is not reproducible from its own source, and a regressed or
      compromised upstream enters the build silently, with nothing in the repository recording what
      was actually used.

      The lock files are committed, and the builds that produce published artifacts restore in
      locked mode so a graph change has to arrive as a deliberate commit rather than as a surprise
      between two builds of the same source. Two mechanisms, because neither reaches everywhere:
      the RestoreLockedMode condition below covers any restore on a CI runner, and the switch is
      passed explicitly on each pack in release.yml and on the restores in Dockerfile and
      Dockerfile.migration — a docker build does not inherit the runner's CI=true, so the condition
      alone left the published images uncovered.

      Locked mode is deliberately NOT applied to demos/custom-server: it floats Authagonal.* at
      0.*, which resolves to something new on every release by design, so it would fail on every
      run. Take an intended graph change with the restore switch that forces re-evaluation.

      Regenerate lock files on a machine whose package cache does NOT hold the SDK's own copy of
      Microsoft.NET.ILLink.Tasks. IsTrimmable pulls that package in, the SDK ships a copy of it in
      library-packs, and NuGet.Config's packageSourceMapping does not map that folder — so a clean
      machine takes it from nuget.org, whose .nupkg is a different file with a different content
      hash. A lock file written against the SDK-local copy fails NU1403 on every clean restore,
      which is a hard error even without locked mode, and that is precisely the machine every
      release and every docker build runs on. Delete the cached entry and restore again if a lock
      file starts disagreeing with CI about that package.
    -->
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>true</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>

    <!--
      Locked mode, on CI only.

      A committed lock file alone changes nothing about what gets built: without locked mode, a
      restore that finds a newer version for a floating range silently re-resolves, REWRITES the lock
      file, and carries on. So the release build on a fresh runner could still pull an unreviewed
      minor or patch of Npgsql, DataProtection or Azure.Storage.Blobs — the exact ingestion the lock
      file was added to stop — and the only evidence would be a dirty file in a workspace nobody
      inspects. Locked mode makes that restore FAIL instead, which is the whole point: the graph
      moves when someone commits a new lock file, not when nuget.org changes under the build.

      CI only, because locally re-resolving is the normal way to take a dependency bump; the
      developer then commits the resulting lock file and CI holds them to it. GitHub Actions sets
      CI=true, and MSBuild surfaces environment variables as properties.
    -->
    <RestoreLockedMode Condition="'$(RestoreLockedMode)' == '' And ('$(CI)' == 'true' Or '$(ContinuousIntegrationBuild)' == 'true')">true</RestoreLockedMode>

    <!--
      One version for the whole Microsoft.IdentityModel family, because a partial pin is worse
      than no pin.

      Server and Protocol pinned Microsoft.IdentityModel.JsonWebTokens and .Tokens at 8.17.0 and
      left Microsoft.IdentityModel.Protocols, .Protocols.OpenIdConnect and
      System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt to whatever Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer
      dragged in — which was 8.0.1, seventeen patch releases behind, inside the same token
      validation pipeline. An 8.0.1 .Protocols.OpenIdConnect fetching an upstream OIDC provider's
      metadata and handing it to an 8.17.0 validator means every hardening added to metadata
      retrieval in between is missing from the path that decides whether a federated id_token is
      trustworthy. Microsoft's own guidance is to keep the family on one version.

      Referencing $(IdentityModelVersion) everywhere makes that structural: the next advisory in
      this family is one edit, and no sibling can be forgotten and silently float back to whatever
      a transitive dependency asks for.

      8.19.2 is the version ASP.NET Core 9.0.18 / 10.0.10 themselves depend on. The number moves
      with the JwtBearer floor and for the same reason: holding the family below what JwtBearer
      requires is a package downgrade, and holding it above what Microsoft shipped against means
      running a validation pipeline Microsoft did not test. One edit here, both legs.
    -->
    <IdentityModelVersion>8.19.2</IdentityModelVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
